Course.start({"num":2,"unit":1,"slug":"m02-data-collection","minutes":40,"title":"How Data Is Collected and Used","subtitle":"Cookies, trackers, data brokers, targeted ads, and what algorithms infer about you","lede":"Many of the apps you use every day are free. They still make money, often billions of dollars, by collecting data about you and using it to sell ads. This module shows how that data is collected, where it goes, and what computers can figure out from it.","recap":"Last time you learned that metadata can reveal private things, and that your footprint has an active part (what you share) and a passive part (what's collected about you).","standards":{"csta":["3A-IC-24","3A-IC-29"]},"context":{"title":"The coupons that knew","place":"Minneapolis, Minnesota · 2012","text":"In 2012, a *New York Times Magazine* article called \"How Companies Learn Your Secrets\" described how the store **Target** studied its customers' shopping.\n\nTarget gave each shopper an ID number linked to their credit card, email and purchases. A statistician there looked at the shopping of customers who had signed up for a baby registry, and found about **25 products** whose pattern could predict a pregnancy, and even roughly estimate the due date. Customers who suddenly bought **unscented lotion**, certain **vitamin supplements** and **large bags of cotton balls** were often pregnant. Target used a \"pregnancy prediction score\" to send those customers coupons for baby items.\n\nThe article told the story of a father near Minneapolis who walked into a Target, angry that his teenage daughter was being mailed coupons for cribs and baby clothes. A few days later, he called back to apologize: his daughter was pregnant, and the store's computer had figured it out before he knew.\n\nShe had never told Target anything. The algorithm **inferred** it."},"learn":[{"title":"If it's free, how does it make money?","text":"Many free apps and websites make most of their money from **targeted advertising**: ads chosen for you based on data about you.\n\nWhen you open an app, your **profile** (age, location, interests, what you've searched) can be offered to advertisers in an instant **auction**. Advertisers bid to show you their ad, and the winner's ad appears. This is called **real-time bidding**, and it happens billions of times a day.\n\n**The more detailed the profile, the more the ad space is worth.** That gives companies a reason to collect as much data as they can.","after":"You'll hear: *\"If you're not paying for the product, you are the product.\"* It's not true of every free service (some run on donations or paid upgrades), but it explains a lot of the internet."},{"title":"The tracking toolkit","table":{"head":["Tool","What it does","Think of it as…"],"rows":[["**First-party cookie**","A small file the site you're visiting saves to remember you (logins, your cart)","A store's membership card"],["**Third-party cookie**","Set by a *different* company inside the site, often an advertiser, to follow you across many sites","A stranger following you from store to store, taking notes"],["**Tracking pixel**","A tiny invisible image in a webpage or email that reports when it's opened","A hidden camera that tells the sender \"they opened it at 7:42 p.m.\""],["**Device fingerprinting**","Identifying your device from details like screen size, fonts, browser version and time zone","Recognizing someone by their walk and voice, with no name tag"],["**App permissions**","Access to your camera, microphone, contacts or location","Giving someone a key to a room in your house"],["**Data broker**","A company that buys, combines and sells profiles of people","Someone who buys everyone's notes and sells the combined file"]]},"after":"Many browsers now block third-party cookies, so companies have shifted to other methods like fingerprinting and tracking people who are logged in. **The tools change; the reason for collecting stays the same.**"},{"title":"Inference: what the algorithm figures out","text":"An **algorithm** is a step-by-step set of instructions. Companies use algorithms to find **patterns** in data and make **inferences**: conclusions about you that you never shared.\n\nA simple prediction rule might look like this:\n\n> IF a customer suddenly buys moving boxes AND packing tape AND a new shower curtain THEN predict \"moving soon\"\n\nRecommendation feeds work the same way, comparing what you do with what millions of similar people do.\n\nInferences can be **wrong** (maybe you were buying for someone else). They can be **sensitive**: health, money, religion, relationships. And you usually **can't see or correct** them."}],"tasks":[{"id":"t1","type":"match","title":"The tracking toolkit","pairs":[["third-party cookie","lets another company follow you across many websites"],["tracking pixel","an invisible image that reports when you open an email"],["fingerprinting","identifies your device by its unique settings"],["data broker","buys, combines and sells profiles of people"],["first-party cookie","keeps you logged in on the site you're visiting"]]},{"id":"t2","type":"sort","title":"Does the app need it?","intro":"Does the permission make sense for what the app does?","cats":["Makes sense","Suspicious"],"items":[["A map app asking for your location",0],["A video chat app asking for your camera",0],["A photo editor asking for your photos",0],["A voice memo app asking for your microphone",0],["A flashlight app asking for your contacts",1],["A calculator app asking for your location",1],["A wallpaper app asking for your microphone",1],["A simple puzzle game asking to read your texts",1]]},{"id":"t3","type":"steps","title":"Inside an ad auction","intro":"Put the steps of real-time bidding in order. It all happens in less than a second.","items":["You open an app or a webpage","Your profile is sent to an ad exchange","Advertisers' computers bid to show you an ad","The highest bidder wins","The winning ad appears on your screen"],"hints":["The bidding can't start until the advertisers know who's looking."]},{"id":"t4","type":"read","title":"Build a prediction rule","intro":"You're a data scientist at SnackStream, an online snack store. Your boss wants to show sports-drink ads to athletes.","passage":"**Your rule:**\n\n> IF a customer buys protein bars AND sports drinks THEN predict \"athlete\"\n\n**Five customers:**\n\n- **C1** buys protein bars and sports drinks, and is an athlete.\n- **C2** buys chips and energy drinks, and is not an athlete.\n- **C3** buys sports drinks and fruit, and is an athlete.\n- **C4** buys protein bars, sports drinks, chips and diapers every week for a family of six, and is not an athlete.\n- **C5** buys only fruit, and is not an athlete.","questions":[{"q":"How many of the five customers does the rule get right?","choices":[["3 of 5",true,"Right on C1, C2 and C5. Wrong on C3 (missed) and C4 (wrongly labeled)."],["5 of 5",false],["4 of 5",false],["1 of 5",false]]},{"q":"Why did the rule label C4 an athlete?","choices":[["C4 buys for a whole family, so the pattern fits even though the inference is wrong",true],["C4 is secretly an athlete",false],["The rule is perfect",false],["C4 never buys sports drinks",false]]},{"q":"Suppose the label were \"likely to have diabetes\" and it was used to raise someone's insurance price. Why would wrong guesses matter more?","choices":[["Because the inference is about sensitive health information and could cause real harm",true],["They wouldn't matter at all",false],["Because diabetes labels are more accurate",false],["Because insurance companies can't use data",false]]}]},{"id":"t5","type":"quiz","title":"What's going on here?","questions":[{"q":"You look at sneakers on one website, and then see ads for the same sneakers on three other sites. What is most likely happening?","choices":[["Third-party trackers or an ad network are following you across sites",true],["The sneakers are very popular",false],["Your phone is broken",false],["The websites are owned by the same person",false]]},{"q":"A company you've never used sends you mail with your name, age and hobbies. How might it have gotten them?","choices":[["It bought a profile from a data broker",true],["It guessed randomly",false],["The post office gave it your hobbies",false],["That's impossible",false]]},{"q":"Your video app keeps recommending sad videos after you watched a few. What's the algorithm doing?","choices":[["Showing you more of whatever kept you watching, based on patterns",true],["Reading your mind",false],["Choosing at random",false],["Following a rule set by your teacher",false]]}]},{"id":"t6","type":"cloze","title":"Key ideas","intro":"Fill in the missing word.","items":["Ads chosen for you based on data about you are called [targeted] ads.","A company that buys, combines and sells profiles of people is a data [broker].","A conclusion an algorithm draws about you from patterns is an [inference].","Target's algorithm predicted pregnancy from about [25|twenty-five] products.","Advertisers bid to show you an ad in a real-time [auction]."]}],"build":{"heading":"A rule for H.E.R.B.I.E.","prompt":"In *Fantastic Four: First Steps*, the family has **H.E.R.B.I.E.**, a robot helper that works around their home and helps care for the baby. A helper like that would see and hear everything.\n\nWrite **at least 5 sentences** describing the **data rules** you'd want for a home robot or a real smart device (a smart speaker, home camera or baby monitor). Name **two kinds of data** it would collect, one **benefit** of collecting it, one **risk**, and **two rules** about what it can store or share.","model":"A home robot like H.E.R.B.I.E. would collect audio of everything said in the house and video of the baby. One benefit is that it could warn the parents right away if the baby stopped breathing. One risk is that the recordings could be hacked or sold to advertisers. My first rule is that it should delete recordings after 24 hours unless the parents save them. My second rule is that it can never share data with other companies without the family's permission.","placeholder":"A home robot would collect…","reqs":[{"t":"At least 5 sentences","sentences":5},{"t":"Two kinds of data","min":2,"any":["audio","video","voice","voices","location","photos","images","recordings","conversations","camera","microphone","temperature","sleep","heart rate","health","schedule","faces","movements"]},{"t":"A benefit","any":["benefit","helpful","help","helps","safe","safety","warn","useful","protect","convenient"]},{"t":"A risk","any":["risk","danger","dangerous","hacked","hack","leak","leaked","sold","sell","share","creepy","spy","misused"]},{"t":"Two rules","min":2,"any":["rule","rules","should","must","never","only","can't","cannot","delete","permission","not allowed"]}]},"reflect":{"q":"The Target algorithm learned something deeply personal about a teenager before her own family knew. Should companies be allowed to make inferences like that? Where would you draw the line, and why?","placeholder":"I think companies should…"},"further":{"id":"x1","type":"quiz","title":"Challenge: reading the fine print","questions":[{"q":"A 2008 study by Carnegie Mellon researchers estimated how long it would take an average person to read the privacy policy of every website they visit in a year. About how long?","choices":[["About 200 hours, or 76 eight-hour workdays",true],["About 2 hours",false],["About 20 minutes",false],["About 10 years",false]]},{"q":"Why might a company make its Terms of Service very long and hard to read?","choices":[["Fewer people read them, so fewer people object to how data is used",true],["The law requires 50 pages",false],["Short policies are illegal",false],["To help users understand them",false]]}]},"culture":"🦸 **Film note:** In *Fantastic Four: First Steps*, Reed Richards is a scientist whose lab is full of scanners, monitors and instruments, and the family shares its home with **H.E.R.B.I.E.**, a robot helper. As you watch, notice **who is collecting information about whom**, and whether the person being studied gets a say.","unitTitle":"Your Data","code":"Mzk1Mi1SRUtDQVJU","total":7,"prev":"m01-digital-footprint.html","next":"m03-protecting-data.html"});
